-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- NOTES
-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- 1. ocr_result and document_verification are both 1:N against
--    document_upload, not 1:1 - a document can be OCR'd more than once
--    (retry after a FAILED attempt, e.g. document_id 3) and can be
--    verified more than once (e.g. an initial MANUAL_REVIEW followed
--    by a final decision). Always query the LATEST row per document_id
--    (ORDER BY processed_at DESC / verified_at DESC LIMIT 1) rather
--    than assuming one row per document.
-- 2. name_match_score / dob_match compare ocr_result's extracted fields
--    against customers.first_name + last_name + date_of_birth. This
--    comparison logic belongs in the service layer (fuzzy name
--    matching, nickname handling, etc.) - the database only stores the
--    resulting score, it doesn't compute it.
-- 3. file_path stores a reference (e.g. an S3 key), not the actual
--    file - this database does not store document images/PDFs
--    directly. Actual file storage (S3, local disk, etc.) is a
--    separate concern from this schema.
-- 4. When document-ocr-service (Java) is built, its @Table names must
--    match document_upload, ocr_result, document_verification exactly,
--    and its enums must match the CHECK constraint values above - same
--    class of integration bug already flagged for account-service.
